{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-04-23T01:02:20.411Z","sessionId":"e72f3c16-a73d-4eb6-a92d-4bfeb62a91b9","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a841e0cec2f313046</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01Tsjiv1awvChGuWsCXpjBH8</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-pfleidi-entire-cli--worktrees-feat-checkpoints-v2-close-test-gaps/e72f3c16-a73d-4eb6-a92d-4bfeb62a91b9/tasks/a841e0cec2f313046.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Correctness &amp; quality review\" completed</summary>\n<result>No prior reviews. Now I have gathered all the evidence needed. Let me compile my findings.\n\n---\n\nHere is the code review for the `feat/checkpoints-v2-close-test-gaps` branch.\n\n## Review Findings\n\n### Critical (Must Fix)\n\nNo critical issues found. The branch adds well-structured test coverage for checkpoints v2 modes across unit, integration, and E2E test layers. The code is correct and follows existing patterns consistently.\n\n### Important (Should Fix)\n\n**1. [e2e/tests/attach_test.go:72] `show-ref --verify` with short ref is fragile in legacy mode**\n\nThe code uses `testutil.CheckpointMetadataRef()` with `show-ref --verify`, which requires a full ref path. In legacy mode, `CheckpointMetadataRef()` returns `entire/checkpoints/v1` (a short ref, not `refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1`), so `show-ref --verify` will always fail. In v2 mode, `refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/main` is a full ref and works correctly.\n\n```go\nif _, refErr := testutil.GitOutputErr(s.Dir, \"show-ref\", \"--verify\", \"--hash\", testutil.CheckpointMetadataRef()); refErr == nil {\n```\n\nIn practice this is correct because in legacy mode the ref genuinely does not exist yet at this point in the test (it's before the first commit that creates a checkpoint). But the code is misleading -- it appears to detect \"ref exists\" but actually always fails in legacy mode for the wrong reason.\n\nSuggested fix: Use `rev-parse --verify` instead of `show-ref --verify`, since `rev-parse` resolves short refs. Or prepend `refs/heads/` in the legacy case.\n\n**2. [e2e/testutil/repo.go:190-198] `checkpointsMode()` default case is redundant and masks invalid input**\n\n```go\nfunc checkpointsMode() string {\n    switch mode := os.Getenv(\"E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE\"); mode {\n    case \"\", checkpointsModeLegacy:\n        return checkpointsModeLegacy\n    case checkpointsModeV2DualWrite, checkpointsModeV2Only:\n        return mode\n    default:\n        return mode // silently passes through unknown values\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe `default` arm returns unknown modes as-is. `ApplySuiteCheckpointsMode()` catches these downstream with `t.Fatalf()`, but `primaryCheckpointRef()` silently falls back to v1 on unknown modes. This inconsistency means an invalid `E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE` value would fail in `ApplySuiteCheckpointsMode` during setup but silently use v1 refs in `primaryCheckpointRef` if that function were ever called before setup. The behavior is currently safe because setup always runs first, but the inconsistency is a maintenance hazard.\n\nSuggested fix: Have `checkpointsMode()` return only known values and add a panic or explicit error for unknown modes, or add validation to `primaryCheckpointRef()` matching the other functions.\n\n### Suggestions (Consider)\n\n**3. [e2e/tests/clean_test.go:74-88] `shadowBranches` duplicates unexported function from testutil/assertions.go**\n\nThe `shadowBranches` function in `clean_test.go` is nearly identical to the unexported `shadowBranches` in `testutil/assertions.go` (lines 123-135). Both filter `for-each-ref` output the same way. The duplication exists because the `testutil` version is unexported.\n\nSuggested fix: Export `ShadowBranches` in `testutil/assertions.go` and call it from `clean_test.go` to eliminate the duplication.\n\n**4. [e2e/agents/vogon.go:145-152] `vogonHomeDir` creates temp directories without cleanup**\n\n`vogonHomeDir` creates directories under `/tmp/vogon-e2e-home-&lt;hash&gt;` that are never cleaned up. While the hash is deterministic (same repo dir = same home), these accumulate across test runs. The `attach_test.go` tests correctly use `t.TempDir()` for managed HOME dirs, but `RunPrompt` and `StartSession` use `vogonHomeDir` which leaks.\n\nSuggested fix: Register a cleanup function in the calling test, or at minimum document that these directories persist across runs.\n\n**5. [e2e/testutil/repo.go:712-722] `NewCheckpointCommits` still hardcodes `entire/checkpoints/v1`**\n\nSeveral utility functions in `repo.go` and `assertions.go` still hardcode `entire/checkpoints/v1` (`NewCheckpointCommits`, `CheckpointIDs`, `ReadCheckpointMetadata`, `ReadSessionMetadata`, `AssertCheckpointExists`, `AssertCheckpointInLastN`, `ValidateCheckpointDeep`, etc.). These are not called from the tests added in this branch but will break when existing tests (e.g., `multi_session_test.go`, `split_commits_test.go`, `stash_workflows_test.go`) are run with `E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE=v2-only`. This is likely known tech debt but worth flagging -- running the full E2E suite with `v2-only` mode will fail on many existing tests due to these hardcoded refs.\n\n**6. [e2e/tests/doctor_test.go:44] `os.Getenv` instead of helper function**\n\nThe doctor test switches on `os.Getenv(\"E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE\")` directly rather than using the `testutil.checkpointsMode()` helper. This means the empty-string normalization to \"legacy\" is duplicated (the switch treats `\"\"` and `\"legacy\"` as the same case).\n\n```go\nswitch os.Getenv(\"E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE\") {\ncase \"\", \"legacy\":\n```\n\nThis works correctly but bypasses the abstraction. If the env var name or default behavior changes, this would need a separate update. Consider exposing a function from `testutil` that returns the mode, or accepting the duplication since the doctor test specifically needs to know the exact string values for mode-specific assertions.\n\n### Praise\n\n- The `mergeSettings` / `cloneSettingsMap` implementation in `repo.go` (lines 635-662) is well-designed -- it correctly handles nested map merging (e.g., `strategy_options` inside settings) without clobbering sibling keys. This fixes a real problem where `PatchSettings` with a flat map assignment would overwrite unrelated strategy options.\n\n- The `ensureHookEntireBinary` function in `main_test.go` (lines 84-102) is a clean solution to the problem of git hooks not finding the test binary. The `filepath.Clean` comparison to handle the already-correct case is a nice defensive touch.\n\n- The `WriteSessionTranscript` method on Vogon (agents/vogon.go lines 97-143) is well-separated from the prompt execution path. The `--write-session` flag with proper validation in `parseArgs` keeps the Vogon binary's concerns clean.\n\n- Good use of `CommitIfDirty` in `resume_remote_test.go` to handle idempotent `entire enable` -- this prevents flaky test failures when enable produces no repo changes in clone scenarios.\n\n- The `ApplySuiteCheckpointsMode` / `PushCheckpointRefs` / `primaryCheckpointRef` abstraction layer is well-structured. It centralizes the mode-specific behavior so new E2E tests don't need to know about ref naming differences between v1 and v2.\n\n- Consistent use of `testutil.WaitForCheckpoint` and `testutil.WaitForCheckpointAdvanceFrom` (which now use `primaryCheckpointRef()`) ensures existing tests automatically adapt to the active checkpoint mode.\n\n### Summary\n\n- Overall assessment: **APPROVE**\n- The branch adds substantial test coverage for checkpoints v2 modes (dual-write and v2-only) across all three test layers: unit tests (`attach_test.go`), integration tests (`v2_dual_write_test.go`, `v2_resume_test.go`), and E2E tests (attach, clean, doctor, explain, resume). The code is well-structured, follows existing patterns, and correctly abstracts the mode-specific behavior behind helper functions.\n- Key concern: The `show-ref --verify` with short ref (finding #1) is the most concrete issue, though its impact is limited since the test outcome is still correct. The other findings are code quality improvements rather than correctness bugs.\n- Estimated effort to address feedback: 30 minutes for findings #1 and #2, optional for the rest.</result>\n<usage><total_tokens>115801</total_tokens><tool_uses>48</tool_uses><duration_ms>243432</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
